A||||44||Ferrari_cdn||performance of a caddy v8?||||6||
Z||000000||Ferrari_cdn||10-25-2001||03:32 AM||||Was having a nice long chat with a forum member about swapping my 4cyl motor to something else, and we seem to be discussing a lot to swap with a 4.5L or a 4.9L caddy V8.  He said it would have about 270 ft/lbs torque (apparently sick acceleration  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">), 200 hp (4.9L).  We couldn't keep dicsussing more since it was already 3 in the morning and got classes in the morning  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>Although we have not actually decided on that motor yet, I would like to know what the performance of that engine would give me (0-60, 1/4 miles, how it compares to other motors, top speed and etc).<P>Any info would be greatly appreciated, and thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">  </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||6
Z||000001||Rick 88||10-25-2001||05:16 PM||||I can't give you any numbers, but I have driven a 4.9 Caddy recently. This engine is designed to move that car off the line quite easily. With the 4-speed auto the Caddy will keep up with quite a few sporty cars. I imagine in a Fiero it should be an eye opener because of the great low speed torque. It will be effortless power in a Fiero when matched with the automatic, and should offer fantastic passing power as well.<P>We are only waiting on Ed's kit to plug this setup into an 88 GT.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Rick 88||||reg||7
Z||000002||Darren's 87 coupe||10-25-2001||06:24 PM||||hey I just got off the phone with another member about the same topic.  He convinced me to at least look into the 4.9l  It really sounds like an easier upgrade than most and the numbers sound really sweet coming from an old iron duke.  I was just going to rebuild that, but he says that the upgrade isn't very expensive for what you get out of it.  I am interested in other input as well, so try to keep this thread running for a while if any one drops in that has this.  Let us know prices difficulty level, are you happy you did it....etc.  thanks<BR>Darren||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ferrari_cdn||10-26-2001||12:37 AM||||I was told it is not supposed to be a hard job, and that it is not expensive as well.  With 270 ft/lbs of torque, it's no question that off the line acceleration would be something.  Would be needing new rear tires often.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">  </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||10
Z||000004||lou_dias||10-26-2001||07:10 PM||||Well, a stock 4.9 puts out 205-215hp.<BR>When I do mine (in 6 months or so), I will rebuild it (should be a 5.0liter then) porting the heads and intake.<BR>Also, I will deck the heads and block to bump up compression a half a point and will look for a cam.<P>My goal is 265hp and a 0-60 of 4.9...||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||10-26-2001||07:57 PM||||Anyone heard of ANY 4.9 performance parts?<P>I'm guessing there's not a huge market for hopped up Devilles.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Cam may need to be a custom weld and grind. There should be some springs out there somewhere that would fit. Pistons are probably a no-go unless something else happens to fit.||||reg||1
Z||000006||jimmybpei||10-26-2001||08:29 PM||||As discussed in another thread on this very topic i just finished mine this week and figure i ony have about 20-25 hours into it.<P>thas pulling the fiero motor on the floor (no hoist) by myself, preping the caddy and installing it. not too hard at all as this was my first one (4.5) <P>As for cost the bars minimun woas about 230.00 can funds thats for bubbajoes mounts and 2 poly bushings. <P>Pretty happy with the job and every one that sees it say the same thing "it looks like it supposed to be in there, look at the stock dent in the valve cover for the trunk hindg"<P>So easy i plan on doing a 4.9 fast back and addin NOS to it.<P>Hope this will help you make up your mind<P>Later Jimmy B.||||reg||1
Z||000007||westtexas||10-27-2001||09:50 AM||||Jimmy, <P>You are a first class mechanic to work that fast.  Did you use a carb or fuel injection? Auto or standard? Did you use a torque strap? Can you light the tires up at will in the lower gears? Please share your experience with the rest of us.<P>Not a great mechanic,<BR>Robert<p>[This message has been edited by westtexas (edited 10-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||westtexas||10-27-2001||12:31 PM||||Jimmy,<P>Did you spend 25 hours swapping the engine into the engine bay or did you spend the same time actually getting it able to drive on the road and pass inspection? What did you do for the idler pulley? What kind of exhaust did you weld up? Some pictures would be great. If it's diveable some 0 to 60 times answer the threads question.<P>Thanks,<BR>Robert||||reg||1
Z||000009||isthiswhereiputausername?||10-27-2001||01:10 PM||||I  think I need to convince the Wife I *need* a caddy motor in ours  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>Jimmy, is yours standard or automatic?<P>Also is there any wrecked caddys up your way? Dont see any up here..<P>Any other cars had the 4.5?<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||gladiator||10-27-2001||11:30 PM||||I'm seriously considering this engine for my 86. As far as performance parts, there is a company here in Indianapolis that builds up the 4.9 to around 250 hp (I believe) and they rebuild the tranny as well. They mainly advertise it for swapping into a W-Body or for the Caddy but they were aware that the engine has been swapped into Fieros. I think their web site is: asgindy.com<BR>I've been on it but there isn't much there. I'll try to find their business card and post that info in a little bit.||||reg||1
Z||000011||gladiator||10-28-2001||12:48 AM||||Now that I have a little more time, here is the info I promised:<BR>Automotive Service Group (atGeist)<BR>5841 Thunderbird Rd<BR>Suite 3<BR>Indianapolis, IN 46236<BR>(317)826-2906 <A HREF="http://www.asgindy.com" TARGET=_blank>www.asgindy.com</A> <BR>The guy you want to talk with is Adam Goldstein.<BR>They're basically a small repair shop. They developed the "5.0 Engine Package" to replace the 2.8 v6 found in GM W bodies and as a performance option for the heavier Cadillacs. The guy I talked to at the front desk (not Adam) said they install only and that they didn't offer this as a kit (heavy sigh). Their camshaft may be available separately, though. Who knows, if enough people call them and show some serious interest, they may work with us. As far as other sources for the 4.x engines, I'd try doing some searches under 'Cadillac Performance' etc.  I found ASG through some guy's site on his Seville after doing a few searches. There also used to be a company that rebuilds these motors. The name escapes me, but you can see their ad in the articles on rebuilding the Cadillac 4.x on the Fiero Secrets website. They may have info on H-P parts. If anyone finds anything out, please let the rest of us know.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||westtexas||10-28-2001||09:02 AM||||I have BubbaJoe's mounts and his two barrel 2CG carb adapter. You need to be aware that my parts were not made with precision. I had to elongate the block side holes on both mounts to get them to fit. The carb adapter air flow holes are off enough the there is a 1/4' gap between the manifold and adapter. I was disappointed that the engine mounts were welded on just one side the triangular reinforcement. He also seems to have abandoned his Fiero Caddy Swap Group. I think that you can do a swap fairly cheaply using his methods/parts although having a welded/redrilled flywheel spinning at 6,000 rpm behind your back may take some faith in the infinite strength of metals. BubbaJoe is an old fashioned hot rodder using salvage yard knowledge to improve his car. I admire him for that. He said on his forum that older Olds V-8 higher lift rockers will fit on the 4.9. That's cheap HP. Another good point he brought out: the earlier exhaust manifolds are made of steel and you can weld to them to make your own custom headers. He stated that advancing the cam timing will make a difference. According to Bubba, his 4.9 dynoed at 267 HP. I haven't heard how his back sugery came out but I hope he is doing well. I guess you get what you pay for. Ed Park's kit will be more expensive but you know that he will deliver first class parts and service. For mechanically impaired people like me, it's the way to go.<P>Jimmy, have you started your engine yet? Your silence makes me a wonder. The thread started with a question: how fast is the 4.9 swap? You never told us how fast yours is.<P>regards,<BR>Robert<P><p>[This message has been edited by westtexas (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfixit58||10-29-2001||08:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by westtexas:<BR><B>BubbaJoe's ... said on his forum that older Olds V-8 higher lift rockers will fit on the 4.9. Robert<P>[This message has been edited by westtexas (edited 10-28-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I would really like to hear more about this. Do you know what he meant by "older"? Are the rockers stock or was he talking about buying performance rockers? What is the ratio of the Olds rockers? For that matter, what is the ratio of the Caddy's?<BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||MinnGreenGT||10-29-2001||05:51 PM||||*bumpity-bump*||||reg||1
Z||000015||westtexas||10-29-2001||06:37 PM||||I went to BubbaJoe's forum and searched for a considerable time but did not find the discussion on rockers. As remember: the Oldsmobile engines were from the sixties and early seventies and ranged from 350 to 425 cubic inches. Using productio Olds rockers changes the ratio from 1.6 to 1.7. I have no direct knowledge of this but supposedly the  Olds rockers will bolt on the 4.9. Joe has  quite a few tricks to increase the HP of the 4.9. The ones that I'm aware of are: using a HEI distributor with a faster advance curve, using a 2 barrel carb that runs a little richer than the FI, advancing the cam 2 degrees (5 is possible)and using 4.1 tube exhaust to build his own headers. He said he was dynoing 267 HP.<P>The SFI 4.9 swap would be an everyday 2.5 replacement if a computer/harness was available. Perhaps, in the future, we'll be able to buy one from Ed Parks. You could get twice the displacement/HP with a bolt-in. Would make a nice commuter.<P>I have a slow 56kps modem. If you want to search Joe's forum it's at: <A HREF="http://communities.msn.ca/FieroCaddySwapGroup/messageboard.msnw" TARGET=_blank>http://communities.msn.ca/FieroCaddySwapGroup/messageboard.msnw</A><BR> <BR>Robert<p>[This message has been edited by westtexas (edited 10-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||rockcrawl||10-29-2001||07:13 PM||||I've never run my car on the strip or dyno, so I don't have any numbers. My 4.5 with port EFI will not roast the tires. It just hooks and goes. It does chirp the tires when it shifts if I have the modulator and trottle vavle set for late, firm shifts. I'm running a 4T60 auto from a '87 Oldsmobile Delta 88 3.8. I'm sure it's got some way high final drive ratio. With a manual trans I bet it could really smoke 'em.<P> I've just recently heard about the cam timing and rocker arm swap. I'm going to try both on my next engine. The stock rockers appear to be 1.5 ratio. The Olds 350 and 455 are supposed to be 1.6 and 1.7, and bolt right on. That plus a few degrees advance on the cam should make a noticeable difference considering the engine was designed to be a fuel efficient smooth runner.....but 67hp??? We'll see. BubbaJoe also claimed he could pull the front tires off the ground in second gear.....  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_bs.gif"> Sounds like he needs new shock/struts. <P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Joe has quite a few tricks to increase the HP of the 4.9....using a HEI distributor with a faster advance curve, using a 2 barrel carb that runs a little richer than the FI<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Or you could run a F-body ECM and keep the efficiency and driveability of the EFI, and be able to make those same changes without picking up a wrench or getting your hands dirty. Carbs suck  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"> no offence, just my opinion.||||reg||7
Z||000017||KRMFiero||10-29-2001||07:27 PM||||rockcrawl, quick question or two, ever do a dry dougnut? or brakestand?   just wondering if when im done i can make some type of sqeeling/smoke from my tires every once in a while<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000018||KRMFiero||10-29-2001||07:32 PM||||anyone know if these will fit my 4.5TBI:<BR>Olds 1964-79 260-455 39* and 45*<BR>1.6 ratio with individual fulcrums and bridge straps<P>should up ugpgrade springs or anything? parts from what engine? <P>also whats the difference in ratios, how much of a effect is 1.6 to 1.7   what does that do (hold valves longer or something?)?||||reg||1
Z||000019||westtexas||10-29-2001||08:53 PM||||"Or you could run a F-body ECM and keep the efficiency and driveability of the EFI, and be able to make those same changes without picking up a wrench or getting your hands dirty. Carbs suck  no offence, just my opinion."<P>You're right, I need to start my education on cars newer than '83. <P>Rockcrawl, just to satisfy my burning curiosity, could you do an informal, hand timed 0-60? <P>Thanks,<BR>Robert||||reg||1
Z||000020||ray b||10-29-2001||09:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KRMFiero:<BR><B>anyone know if these will fit my 4.5TBI:<BR>Olds 1964-79 260-455 39* and 45*<BR>1.6 ratio with individual fulcrums and bridge straps<P>should up ugpgrade springs or anything? parts from what engine? ONLY IF THEY FIX AND I BET THE SPRINGS DONOT JUST THE ROCKER ARMS<BR>HAPPEN TO FIT NOT TO SAY FOR SURE JUST A GUESS<P>also whats the difference in ratios, how much of a effect is 1.6 to 1.7   what does that do (hold valves longer or something?)?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>NOT WHEN IT OPENS BUT HOW FAR THE VALVE MOVES[lift]<BR>BE SURE THERE IS ROOM for extra movement ESP if heads cut to up compression <BR><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000021||rockcrawl||10-29-2001||09:27 PM||||It will do a donut, I've had it sideways a few times when pulling out on a turn, I'm sure if there was room I could keep it going  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> I guess there's enough front brake bias that I could stand on the brakes and roast 'em. Spinning tires doesn't get you anywhere anyway.<BR>The higher ratio rockers will not increase duration, they will increase valve opening. So if the valves open .5" with a 1.5 rocker, they will open .57" with a 1.7 rocker. The amount of time they are open will stay the same.<BR>My Snap-On scanner has a data logging feature that records time and speed (along with lots of other stuff), I'll see what it says for 0-60 next time I have the car out. The slushbox and high gearing are really limiting this thing. I don't think I'll ever build another with an auto.||||reg||1
Z||000022||MinnGreenGT||10-30-2001||09:41 AM||||For those of you who have done this... I'd like to install an injected 4.9L in my '84.  But in an email I recieved from Ed Parks, he doesn't suggest using the '84 due to different wiring locations as well as coolant lines.  Is is worth my time to pick up an 85-87 coupe instead of hassling with the '84? (The '84 is pretty beat as it is).||||reg||1
Z||000023||Philphine||10-30-2001||10:42 AM||||rockcrawl, haven't you done some 4.9's too? can you tell much performance difference? in the injection systems also. i thought i remembered once reading you thought it was worth working out the newer system. i'll be in over my head no matter what i try but i'm wondering if it's worth it to try for the bigger engine and better injection system. ||||reg||1
Z||000024||rockcrawl||10-30-2001||05:08 PM||||The only problem with using an '84 is that the wiring is not as well known. You'd be on your own to do the wiring conversion. The A/C lines are different, but that shouldn't be much of a problem.<P>The '90 4.5 injection system is the same as the 4.9 system, they even use the same ECM. If I showed you a PFI 4.5 next to a 4.9, I garantee you could not tell the difference. The only difference is a longer stroke on the 4.9. The 4.5 PFI was only available in '90 (Alante excluded). The '90 4.5 shares all the impovements of the 4.9 (bigger valves, ports, throttle bores, etc..) but without the longer stroke. I have two 4.9s but one is still in the Caddy and the other isn't running yet. The 4.9 has a factory rating of 200hp, 20 more than the 4.5 running the same injection. 20 hp should be enough to feel i guess, but I still put the '90 4.5 in the same category as the 4.9.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4549.jpg"> <BR>can you tell which is which? OK, without looking at the sticker on the airbox.... Any differences are due to the fact that one is Deville and one is Eldorado. The easiest way to spot a 'good' Caddy engine is the "V8 PFI" on the front valve cover. <p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 10-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||MinnGreenGT||10-30-2001||06:12 PM||||Since my '84 Doesn't have A/C... then the A/C lines are not an issue at all- right?  Just remove the A/C compressor from the engine and all is good.  As to the wiring... Ed mentioned that the plug is in a different location- not that the plug is actually different...?<P>Still not sure if I should go for it on the '84 or just find an '85-'87 with a blown motor.  Might be more than one step ahead if I get a different donor car.<P>BTW: Rockcrawl- you've got a PM.||||reg||1
Z||000026||cadero2dmax||10-30-2001||08:56 PM||||One point being missed here is that the 4.9 complete engine weighs less than 10lbs than the 2.8 equipped the same.<P>And also that in stock form it generates 300ft lbs of torque, peaking at a very low RPM (around 2400, isn't it Rockcrawl?) and has peak torque through almost all of the limited RPM range.<P>First, that is more torque than a lot of 350's, and this thing reminds me of my 24 valve Cummins turbo diesel - gobs of torque from idle up, pulls like a freight train, and has limited RPM.<P>You gotta ride in one to believe that "paltry little 215 hp" can do all that!!<P><P>------------------<BR>G<BR>Daily Driver - <BR>85 SE<BR>4.9 Cad<BR>Isuzu 5 speed<BR>'88 cradle<BR>lowered 2 1/2"<BR>Mr. Mikes interior<P>GRM $2002 Challenge Fiero<BR>84 coupe<BR>4.9 (carburated)<BR>2.86:1 TH-125 w/shift kit||||reg||1
Z||000027||rockcrawl||10-30-2001||09:53 PM||||According to the factory service manual, the 4.9 is 200 hp @ 4100 RPM, 275 lb ft @ 3000 RPM. The Allante is slightly higher, but peaks at 4400 and 3200 RPM due to the different intake design. Most of us will never see an Allante engine in a salvage yard, so it's best to stick to the 200/275 Seville/DeVille/Fleetwood/Eldorado numbers.<BR> A 4.9 in Fiero trim with A/C weighs 371 pounds. 2.8 is 345-376 lb depending who you ask. 3800 series II supercharged a whopping 445 lb. Northstar is 418 lb. Smallblock Chevy 575-586 lb. I assume that's with iron heads.<P>Horsepower sells cars, torque wins races! Somebody here once said that, I forget who.||||reg||7
Z||000028||Philphine||10-30-2001||11:38 PM||||what about hooked up to any of the 4speeds? i think i have all three major ratios right now (i mean the economy and 4:10 and the one in my 86 gt). suppose i got the hang of it and swapped one in all three of my cars. is the redline too low to make having the 4:10 with it be an acceleration advantage? would the difference in gears be enough to give each car a different feel?<P>right now i'd like to put the caddy engine in with the economy 4 speed in my 87 coupe, but if it worked out good i might want to try it again with an '84 i have and use the 4:10 tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000029||mrfixit58||10-31-2001||07:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B> ...right now i'd like to put the caddy engine in with the economy 4 speed in my 87 coupe, but if it worked out good i might want to try it again with an '84 i have and use the 4:10 tranny.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I would think that since the motor makes so much torque in the low RPM range that the economy tranny ratio would be a better fit for street use. You may find that first gear in the 4:10 tranny to be somewhat useless.<P>Just my opinion,<BR>Roy<P>||||reg||1
Z||000030||rockcrawl||10-31-2001||07:45 AM||||I have the 4.10 trans in an '86 SE V6, first gear is almost useless even with the 2.8  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> nah, not really, the problem with the 4.10 is the limited high end, but it's really not much worse than the M17 V6 4 speed. The 4.10 is great for a car that never sees the highway. BubbaJoes 4.9/4.10 pulls wheelies in second gear!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000031||rockcrawl||10-31-2001||05:39 PM||||I had a chance to take the car out and play today. I had a little trouble getting it to launch without wheelspin  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> it must be the cold weather (45 deg). According to the Scanner it runs 0-60MPH in ~6.5 seconds. It shifts 1-2 at 4500 RPM and it's still in second gear at 60, shifts into third around 63MPH. While driving around to get it warmed up I realized there is a dead spot around 2500RPM, I think my last two revisions to the chip made it worse. Why do I always come home with the brakes smoking?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000032||Ferrari_cdn||10-31-2001||09:19 PM||||rockcrawl, I assume you were driving with the 4.9L?<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">  </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000033||cadero2dmax||10-31-2001||11:24 PM||||Rockcrawl<P>My manual shows 275lbft of torque for the 4.5, and 300 for the 4.9<P>But who cares about the numbers. I have experienced the SBC (and was impressed), but the 4.9 is stronger off the line (until it runs out of RPM) and more fun to drive. But I also think it is harder to hook up (because of all that low end grunt). <P>I had a 4.10 4 speed in the SE with the 2.8 before the transplant, let me tell you, you would hate that combo. I would get the economy (3.23, wasn't it) ratio to get more performance (and less tire spin). My 5 speed Isuzu is probably still too tall gearing for the street, I am looking for a cheap LSD (hoping the Phantom Grip works out!).<P>I am putting a 2.86:1 TH125 with a 2400rpm stall convertor (right where this engine hits peak torque) in the Challenge car. The drag times/action will be documented by Grassroots Motorsports Magazine, stay tuned!!<P>G||||reg||1
Z||000034||MinnGreenGT||11-01-2001||01:20 PM||||Hmmm.... so you guys obviously recommend using either auto (I've got the 440T4 4spd OD already installed) over the 4:10 4-speed in my '84SE? I've always understood that the 4:10 would be the best for a drag?  Guess I was mistaken...||||reg||1
Z||000035||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-01-2001||04:36 PM||||the more I read, the more I want one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I have mine converted to 2.8 V6 carbed automatic now and I think I would like to convert the 4.9 to carbed.. would that make the wiring a  lot easier since I have an 84?and no AC so that should save another headache with the ac pipes. <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000036||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-01-2001||04:38 PM||||my first triple post.. Gotta love the dataflow spurts on our work lan  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000037||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-01-2001||04:38 PM||||my first triple post.. Gotta love the dataflow spurts on our work lan  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 11-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000038||Master_Sushi||11-01-2001||06:24 PM||||Whats the redline of a caddie 4.9? From what I hear in this thread its about 5000?||||reg||5
Z||000039||rockcrawl||11-23-2001||09:19 PM||||I just got a G-tech meter. My 4.5 automatic Cadero runs consistent 14.6 quarter mile and 5.9 0-60 with a best run of 14.28 and 5.2. That's with a full tank of fuel and two adults. I will be faster when I get a few minor things taken care of.||||reg||1
Z||000040||Smoooooth GT||11-24-2001||02:02 AM||||Does the 4.x bolt up DIRECTLY to the STOCK Fiero Tran's.????<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Smooooth_Yellow_sig_3.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/texas_1.gif">          <BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
Z||000041||westtexas||11-24-2001||07:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Smoooooth GT:<BR><B>Does the 4.x bolt up DIRECTLY to the STOCK Fiero Tran's.????<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The bellhousing/transmission pattern is identical. The Cad starter is mounted higher on the block. I cut a section of my TH125 bellhousing out to get clearance for the Cad starter nose. You can buy a CSI starter that will bolt in without butchering the Fiero trans bellhousing but I have three autos laying around and I really don't care if one breaks. I'm having fun just messing with them. People with more ambition use 4-speed autos. I understand that Ed Parks has one running with a Cad auto which has to be bullit proof. As far as I can tell you can also bolt right up to a Izusu, Muncie , or Getrag standard. Custom billet flywheels are available. But going standard will increase the cost of the swap after buying the flywheel (somewhere around $400 to $500)and a stronger clutch. IMHO, the 4.9 is a first class 2.5 replacement, it's like having two of them pushing your car around, a little more than twice the horsepower and torque. And it could be cheaper HP than putting parts on a 2.8. But, if you want to really rattle windows, you'll need an Archie kit. <P>Robert<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000042||mrfixit58||11-24-2001||09:34 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>I just got a G-tech meter. My 4.5 automatic Cadero runs consistent 14.6 quarter mile and 5.9 0-60 with a best run of 14.28 and 5.2. That's with a full tank of fuel and two adults. I will be faster when I get a few minor things taken care of.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>So, if you dropped the second adult off at the curb, had 1/4 tank of gas, lose the spare tire, and improve the shift points, 13.x times should easily be attainable. And, this is just the 4.5 liter motor? Sounds like cheap fun!<P>Roy<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000043||Smoooooth GT||11-24-2001||12:17 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by westtexas:<BR><B> The bellhousing/transmission pattern is identical. But going standard will increase the cost of the swap after buying the flywheel (somewhere around $400 to $500)and a stronger clutch. Robert</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>west....... So, Do I need a different flywheel If I use the stock (auto) Fiero transmission??? <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Smooooth_Yellow_sig_3.gif">   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/texas_1.gif">          <BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
Z||000044||westtexas||11-24-2001||12:47 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Smoooooth GT:<BR><B> west....... So, Do I need a different flywheel If I use the stock (auto) Fiero transmission??? <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>My TH125 torque convertor has three bolts that bolt directly to the Cad flex plate. A marriage made in heaven. <P>Robert<BR>||||reg||1
